KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Execute the 'repair' intervention on a production Faiss-HNSW index using SIFT1M (d=128). Identify the lowest-in-degree items across 5 insertion orders. If the blind-spot stability remains <=0.30, OR if adding reverse edges to them fails to beat the recall/latency Pareto frontier of simply increasing ef_search, the phenomenon is an un-actionable curiosity.
RATIONALE: The L0 evaluation rests on a toy Python flat-graph at d=12, which is essentially a strawman for modern vector search. The instability of blind spots across builds (Spearman 0.134) could simply be an artifact of single-layer random entry points, which HNSW's upper layers specifically mitigate. I vote YELLOW: L1 is strictly required to run on a production C++ multi-layer kernel (Faiss/hnswlib) with high-dimensional data (d>=128) to prove this isn't a toy artifact, and it must prove the proposed 'repair' beats the trivial baseline of increasing ef_search.
